(function(){if(!window.__hermes_register_book)return;window.__hermes_register_book("anacreontea-c",[{"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "The Anacreontea"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE I. — ON HIS LYRE."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "While I sweep the sounding string,<br>While th&rsquo; Atridæ&rsquo;s praise I sing,<br>Victors on the Trojan plain,<br>Or to Cadmus raise the strain,<br>Hark! in soft and whisper&rsquo;d sighs,<br>Love&rsquo;s sweet notes the shell replies.<br>Late I strung my harp anew,<br>Changed the strings — the subject too:<br>Loud I sung Alcides&rsquo; toils,<br>Still the lyre my labour foils;<br>Still with Love&rsquo;s sweet silver sounds<br>Every martial theme confounds."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Farewell! heroes, chiefs, and kings,<br>Naught but love will suit my strings."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE II. — ON WOMEN."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Pointed horns, the dread of foes,<br>Nature on the bull bestows;<br>Horny hoofs the horse defend,<br>Swift-wing&rsquo;d feet the hare befriend;<br>Lions&rsquo; gaping jaws disclose<br>Dreadful teeth in grinning rows;<br>Wings to birds her care supplied,<br>Finny fishes swim the tide;<br>Nobler gifts to man assign&rsquo;d,<br>Courage firm and strength of mind.<br>From her then exhausted store<br>Naught for woman has she more:<br>How does Nature prove her care?<br>Beauty&rsquo;s charms is woman&rsquo;s share,"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Stronger far than warrior&rsquo;s dress<br>Is her helpless loveliness.<br>Safety smiles in beauty&rsquo;s eyes,<br>She the hostile flame defies:<br>Fiercest swords submissive fall — <br>Lovely woman conquers all!"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE III. — CUPID BENIGHTED."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "&lsquo;Twas at the solemn midnight hour,<br>When silence reigns with awful pow&rsquo;r,<br>Just when the bright and glittering Bear<br>Is yielding to her keeper&rsquo;s care;<br>When, spent with toil, with cares oppress&rsquo;d,<br>Man&rsquo;s busy race has sunk to rest,<br>Sly Cupid, sent by cruel fate,<br>Stood loudly knocking at my gate.<br>&ldquo;Who&rsquo;s there,&rdquo; I cried, &ldquo;at this late hour?<br>Who is it batters thus my door?<br>Begone! you break my blissful dreams.&rdquo;<br>But he, on mischief bent, it seems,<br>With feeble voice and piteous cries<br>In childish accents thus replies:<br>&ldquo;Be not alarm&rsquo;d, kind sir, &rsquo;tis I,<br>A little, wretched, wandering boy.<br>Pray ope the door — I&rsquo;ve lost my way<br>This moonless night — alone I stray:<br>I&rsquo;m stiff with cold, I&rsquo;m drench&rsquo;d all o&rsquo;er;<br>For pity&rsquo;s sake pray ope the door.&rdquo;<br>Touch&rsquo;d with this simple tale of wo,<br>And little dreaming of a foe,"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "I rose, lit up my lamp, and straight<br>Undid the fastenings of the gate;<br>And there indeed a boy I spied,<br>With bow and quiver by his side.<br>Wings too he wore — a strange attire!<br>My guest I seated near the fire,<br>And while the blazing fagots shine,<br>I chafed his little hands in mine.<br>His dank and dripping locks I wrung,<br>That down his shoulders loosely hung.<br>Soon as his cheeks began to glow,<br>&ldquo;Come now,&rdquo; he cries, &ldquo;let&rsquo;s try this bow<br>For much I fear, this rainy night,<br>The wet and damp have spoil&rsquo;d it quite.&rdquo;<br>That instant twang&rsquo;d the sounding string,<br>Loud as the whizzing gadfly&rsquo;s wing:<br>Too truly aim&rsquo;d, the fatal dart<br>My bosom pierced with painful smart.<br>Up sprung the boy with laughing eyes,<br>And, &ldquo;Wish me joy, mine host,&rdquo; he cries.<br>&ldquo;My bow is sound in ev&rsquo;ry part;<br>Thou&rsquo;lt find the arrow in thy heart.&rdquo;"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE IV. — ON HIMSELF."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "On this flowery couch reclining,<br>Thick with leaves of myrtle strew&rsquo;d,<br>Every graver care resigning,<br>I will drink in joyous mood."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Mis tunic shorten&rsquo;d — standing near me,<br>His waist with rushy girdle bound,<br>With rosy wine let Cupid cheer me,<br>And serve the golden goblet round."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "For, ah! with what unwearied pace<br>The ceaseless wheel of life runs on!<br>Just like the chariot&rsquo;s rapid race,<br>How swift the course, how quickly run!"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Yet thus, alas! our moments fly;<br>Thus pass our fleeting years away;<br>And soon shall we neglected lie,<br>A little dust — a lump of clay!"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Then why, when life&rsquo;s short scene is o&rsquo;er,<br>Anoint a cold unconscious stone?<br>Why vainly rich libations pour,<br>Or call ray ghost with useless moan?"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Nay, rather, friends, anoint me now,<br>While life remains, and fate is kind;<br>With rosy garlands crown my brow,<br>And go, my lovely fair one find."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "My cares I&rsquo;ll drown in pleasure&rsquo;s tide,<br>Before my wand&rsquo;ring spirit go<br>Where unsubstantial spectres glide,<br>And dance in dismal shades below."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE V. — ON THE ROSE."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "With sparkling wine sweet roses join,<br>&lsquo;Twill make the nectar&rsquo;d draught divine;<br>Let mirth and laughter rule the hour,<br>While roses, pluck&rsquo;d from Love&rsquo;s own bower,<br>Around our moisten&rsquo;d temples twine,<br>And add fresh fragrance to the wine."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Oh, lovely rose! to thee I sing,<br>Thou sweetest, fairest child of spring!<br>Oh thou art dear to all the gods,<br>The darling of their bless&rsquo;d abodes.<br>Thy breathing buds and blossoms fair<br>Entwine young Cupid&rsquo;s golden hair,<br>When gayly dancing, hand in hand,<br>He joins the Graces&rsquo; lovely band.<br>Then bring fresh garlands, crown my brows,<br>And while thus joyous, I carouse,<br>Admitted, Bacchus, to thy shrine,<br>Thy praise I&rsquo;ll sing in hymns divine;<br>Or, thick with rosy chaplets crown&rsquo;d,<br>With Chloe dance a sprightly round,<br>Whose snowy bosom softly swells,<br>And tales of tender transport tells."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE VI. — THE BANQUET."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "With glowing wreaths of roses crown&rsquo;d,<br>We&rsquo;ll pass the cheerful goblet round;<br>But with no squeamish, modest sips,<br>The cup shall kiss our thirsty lips.<br>And see, to grace the festive hour,<br>The maiden seeks our shelter&rsquo;d bower,<br>Whose pretty, slender foot well suits<br>The music of the soft-toned lutes;<br>While ivy wreath&rsquo;d, her thyrsus fair<br>She rustles through the yielding air.<br>And hark! a fair-hair&rsquo;d youth begins,<br>And as he wakes the warbling strings"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "His liquid voice breathes odours round,<br>And mingles with the melting sound.<br>With golden locks, young Cupid see,<br>And Bacchus, young and fair as he;<br>With these is lovely Venus too,<br>Who hastes to join the sportive crew;<br>While we old men can scarce refrain<br>To live the life we loved again."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE VII. — ON CUPID."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Cupid once, with staff in hand,<br>(A slender hyacinthine wand,)<br>Slow walking with a tottering pace,<br>Defied me to the rapid race.<br>Away we flew o&rsquo;er flood and fell,<br>O&rsquo;er craggy rock and bushy dell,<br>Till hastening on with swiftest speed,<br>A serpent stung me; then indeed<br>My heart forgot its wonted play;<br>I fainted — sunk — and died away."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "The urchin laughed at my disgrace,<br>And while his pinions fann&rsquo;d my face,<br>&ldquo;My friend,&rdquo; he cried, &ldquo;you clearly prove<br>That you are not a match fur Love!&rdquo;"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE VIII. — ON HIS DREAM."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Peaceful slumbering through the night,<br>On a purple couch reclined,<br>Dreams of joy and visions bright<br>Bacchus sent to charm my mind."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Methought I join&rsquo;d in rapid race<br>With flying nymphs a sportive crew,<br>And urging on with swiftest pace,<br>Still kept the lovely game in view."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "While youths, as young Lyæus fair,<br>With jealous hate, and envy stung,<br>Who saw my joy, but could not share<br>Reviled me as I pass&rsquo;d along."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "A kiss I claim&rsquo;d — my promised prize;<br>But as on pleasure&rsquo;s brink I seem,<br>The vision fled my cheated eyes:<br>I woke, and lo! &rsquo;twas all a dream!"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Then lonely, sad, and angry too,<br>To find my high-raised hopes were vain,"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "The dear delusion to renew,<br>I sigh&rsquo;d, and sunk to sleep again."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE IX. — ON A DOVE."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Pretty pigeon, tell me, pray,<br>Whither speeding, whence away?<br>Breathing balmy odours round,<br>Where thy fluttering pinions sound?<br>Who despatch&rsquo;d thee through the air?<br>What commission dost thou bear?<br>&ldquo;Anacreon, the blithe and gay,<br>The master dear whom I obey,<br>Sent me swift from yonder grove<br>To seek the lady of his love.<br>I dare not tell the name she bears,<br>But beauty&rsquo;s sweetest smile she wears:<br>Possess&rsquo;d of every pleasing art,<br>She reigns supreme o&rsquo;er every heart.<br>Fair Venus sold me to the bard,<br>A little hymn the fix&rsquo;d reward."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "So now the poet&rsquo;s page am I,<br>His courier through the pathless sky;<br>And sometimes, as you see me now,<br>The bearer of some tender vow.<br>He thinks, perhaps, he pleases me,<br>By saying I shall soon be free;<br>But though I should the boon obtain,<br>His willing slave I&rsquo;ll still remain.<br>For, ah! I do not wish to roam,<br>Or quit my sweet, my happy home,<br>Far flying over hill and plain<br>My wretched, rustic food to gain;<br>Or shivering on some tree to stay,<br>And coo the cheerless hours away:<br>For now I feast on dainty bread,<br>And by the hands I love am fed;<br>And when the cup has pressed his lip,<br>His sweet delicious wine I sip;<br>And when my heart is light and gay,<br>I sometimes little frolics play;<br>Upon his shoulder take my place,<br>And with my wings o&rsquo;erspread his face.<br>Or if to sleep my humour suit,<br>I perch upon his warbling lute,<br>And by his careful hand caress&rsquo;d,<br>By softest sounds am lull&rsquo;d to rest.<br>I&rsquo;ve told you all — begone! adieu!<br>And let me now my flight pursue.<br>Nay, friend, no longer urge my stay,<br>For I have prated like a jay.&rdquo;"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE X. — ON A WAXEN CUPID."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "A waxen Cupid, nicely wrought,<br>A rustic youth for sale had brought.<br>&ldquo;Say, what&rsquo;s your price, my friend?&rdquo; I cried,<br>When thus the silly clown replied,<br>In Doric phrase, devoid of skill:<br>&ldquo;E&rsquo;en take him, sir, for what you will:<br>&rsquo;Tis cheap, you&rsquo;ll say; but, truth to tell,<br>No images I make or sell.<br>But as for this young rogue you see,<br>He must not — shall not dwell with me.&rdquo;<br>&ldquo;If so, my pretty youth,&rdquo; I said,<br>&ldquo;Our bargain shall be quickly made:<br>To you this little coin I&rsquo;ll give,<br>And, Cupid, thou with me shalt live.<br>And do thou now my breast inspire,<br>There kindle all thy former fire;<br>Oh let me boast a lover&rsquo;s name,<br>Or thou thyself shalt melt in flame.&rdquo;"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE XI. — ON HIMSELF."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "&ldquo;Anacreon,&rdquo; the lasses say,<br>&ldquo;Old fellow, you have had your day:"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Consult your mirror, mark with care,<br>How scanty now your silver hair;<br>Old wintry Time has shed his snows,<br>And bald and bare your forehead shows.&rdquo;<br>But faith! I know not where they&rsquo;re gone,<br>Or if I&rsquo;ve any left — or none;<br>But this I know, that every day<br>Shall see me sportive, blithe, and gay;<br>For &rsquo;tis our wisdom so to do<br>The nearer death appears in view."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE XII. — ON A SWALLOW."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "What punishment shall I decree,<br>Vexatious, chattering bird, to thee?<br>Say, shall I clip thy restless wing?<br>Or, like the cruel Thracian king,<br>Tear out that tongue whose noisy scream<br>Has loused me from so sweet a dream?"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "For oh! methought my love was nigh,<br>Till, startled by thy twittering cry,<br>She fled upon the wings of morn,<br>And left me joyless and forlorn."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE XIII. — ON HIMSELF."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Poor Atys, as old poets sing,<br>O&rsquo;er the wild mountains wandering,<br>Degraded from his former state,<br>Cybele&rsquo;s love now turned to hate,<br>With plaintive cries invoked relief,<br>Till madness brought an end to grief.<br>And some who to the waters throng,<br>Of laurell&rsquo;d Phœbus, god of song,<br>At Claros drink the vocal wave,<br>And with prophetic fury rave;<br>Then shall not I when wine inspires,<br>And Chloe&rsquo;s eyes dart love&rsquo;s bright fires,<br>When bathed in sweets, without alloy,<br>And rapt in wild, delirious joy,<br>Refuse a while stern reason&rsquo;s sway,<br>And be as madly wild as they?"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE XIV. — ON CUPID."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Cease, cease the combat, I&rsquo;ll obey,<br>Oh, mighty Love! I own thy sway.<br>Cupid plann&rsquo;d a new campaign,<br>And bade me join his camp again;<br>But I, grown weary of the trade,<br>Like a rebel disobey&rsquo;d.<br>Straight the monarch, much displeased,<br>His dreadful bow and quiver seized,<br>And, wafted on his pinions light,<br>Defied me to the field of fight.<br>Then clad for war, like Peleus&rsquo; son,<br>A corslet bright I buckled on;<br>With ample shield and quivering spear,<br>I waited till the foe drew near.<br>His bow-string twang&rsquo;d — then seized with dread,<br>My courage fail&rsquo;d — I trembling fled.<br>He plied his darts till all were spent;<br>Nor did his anger then relent:<br>Himself he changed into a dart,<br>And shot like lightning through my heart."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Ah me! I felt my life-blood flow;<br>I sunk beneath my conquering foe<br>How vainly then a shield I wear!<br>In vain defensive arms I bear;<br>For victory who can hope to win<br>While fiercely burns the war within?"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE XV. — HAPPY LIFE."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Famed Gyges&rsquo; treasures I could see,<br>From envious thoughts and wishes free.<br>On golden heaps with scorn I frown;<br>I would not wear a monarch&rsquo;s crown.<br>Far other joys and cares are mine,<br>For which such bawbles I resign.<br>To bathe my beard with sweet perfumes,<br>To crown my brows with spring&rsquo;s fresh blooms;<br>These — these are things that claim my care.<br>This day is mine — I&rsquo;ll freely share<br>The joys it brings; for who can know<br>If he shall see the next or no?<br>Then, while thy summer sky is clear,<br>Nor death nor danger hover near,<br>The happy hours of life employ<br>In song, and dance, and festive joy;"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "And let the rattling dice assign<br>The royal honours of the wine,<br>Ere surly Death thy garland tear,<br>Or fell disease with frown severe,<br>Forbid the nectar&rsquo;d juice to sip,<br>And dash the goblet from thy lip."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE XVI. — THE CAPTIVE."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Some sing of Thebes, and some prolong<br>The battle-shouts of Phrygian wars;<br>But I must trill a captive&rsquo;s song,<br>Sigh o&rsquo;er my wounds, and count my scars."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Of conq&rsquo;ring fleets no slave am I,<br>No armies claim me for their prize;<br>But all my foes in ambush lie,<br>And dart their fires from Pyrrha&rsquo;s eyes!"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE XVII. — ON A SILVER BOWL."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Mulciber, thou skilful wright,<br>Carve for me this silver bright;<br>But I do not wish to see<br>Polish&rsquo;d arms or panoply.<br>What are arms or wars to me?"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Carve me out a mighty bowl,<br>That my ever-thirsty soul<br>In the generous juice may steep.<br>Make it very — very deep.<br>On the margin do not trace<br>Uncouth shape or horrid face&rdquo;<br>Grave not there the northern wain<br>Stern Orion, god of rain,<br>Boötes, or the Pleiades;<br>What concern have I with these?<br>Trail thereon the tender vine,<br>There let purple clusters shine;<br>Picture too the god of wine.<br>There let fair-hair&rsquo;d Cupid be,<br>And Bathyllus, fair as he:<br>Make them beautiful and bold,<br>Burnish&rsquo;d high like polish&rsquo;d gold:<br>Let them in one labour join,<br>Treading out the gushing wine."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE XVIII. — ON THE SAME SUBJECT."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Dear artist, take this silver store,<br>And try thy skilful hand once more;<br>Produce a large and handsome bowl<br>To charm my eyes, and cheer my soul.<br>Around its polish&rsquo;d surface bring<br>The flowery pride of purple spring;<br>There let the soft and vernal hours<br>Shed rosy sweets in plenteous showers.<br>Engrave no foreign mystic rite,<br>No marv&rsquo;lous tale that shocks the sight;<br>But draw the gen&rsquo;rous god of wine,<br>Blithe Bacchus, son of Jove divine.<br>Let Venus, love&rsquo;s sweet smiling queen,<br>With youthful Hymen deck the scene:<br>His dread artillery laid aside,<br>Let Cupid mid the Graces glide,<br>As in the sprightly dance they join<br>Beneath the high-imbowering vine,<br>Whose glowing clusters peep between<br>The foliage bright of glossy green.<br>With these a youthful group display,<br>As fair as Phœbus, god of day,<br>Though Phœbus join not in their play."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE XIX. — REASONS FOR DRINKING."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "The earth drinks up the genial rains<br>Which deluge all her thirsty plains;"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "The lofty trees that pierce the sky<br>Drain up the earth and leave her dry;<br>Th&rsquo; insatiate sea imbibes, each hour,<br>The welcome breeze that brings the show&rsquo;r;<br>The sun, whose fires so fiercely burn,<br>Absorbs the wave; and, in her turn,<br>The modest moon enjoys, each night,<br>Large draughts of his celestial light.<br>Then, sapient sirs, pray tell me why,<br>If all things drink, why may not I?"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE XX. — TO HIS MISTRESS."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "On desert Phrygia&rsquo;s silent sands<br>Poor Niobe an image stands;<br>And Pandion&rsquo;s injured child, we know,<br>Still, twittering, tells her tale of wo.<br>But would the gods the change allow,<br>And hear and grant my tender vow,<br>Dear girl! thy mirror I would be,<br>That thou might&rsquo;st always smile on me."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Thy vest I&rsquo;d be, to guard with care<br>Those heaving breasts, and nestle there.<br>Oh! would I were a limpid wave,<br>Thy soft and beauteous limbs to lave;<br>Thy perfumed oil, that I might share<br>The glory of thy golden hair!<br>Or, dearer still, that slender zone,<br>Which makes thy beauties all its own:<br>Thy pearly chain, that shines so fair,<br>But cannot with thy neck compare:<br>Thy very sandal I would be,<br>To kiss the foot that trod on me!"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE XXI. — SUMMER."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Bring, maidens, bring a well-mix&rsquo;d bowl,<br>And let me slake my thirsty soul;<br>For, scorch&rsquo;d beneath this sultry sky,<br>My spirits sink — I faint — I die.<br>This garland, late so fresh and fair,<br>I twined amid my curling hair;<br>But all its faded flow&rsquo;rets now<br>Have wither&rsquo;d on my burning brow.<br>Bring fresher wreaths my head to shade;<br>Bring others still when those shall fade.<br>But, oh! what ease can wine impart <br>When love&rsquo;s fierce flame consumes the heart?"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "In vain to groves or shades I fly,<br>This inward flame will never die!"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE XXII. — THE BOWER."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Haste, my love, this shade to seek,<br>The spreading tree is passing fair,<br>Like clust&rsquo;ring curls on Beauty&rsquo;s cheek,<br>See it waves its wanton hair."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "The streamlet murm&rsquo;ring at our feet<br>⁠Rolls its music through the grove;<br>&rsquo;Tis a scene for lovers meet,<br>Where each object whispers love."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "The tree, the stream, the silent hour,<br>All persuasive, seem to say,"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "&ldquo;Viewing such a lovely bower,<br>Can you pass another way?&rdquo;"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE XXIII. — THE VANITY OF WEALTH."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Could glittering heaps, or golden store,<br>Life preserve, or health restore,<br>Then with ceaseless, anxious pain,<br>Riches I would strive to gain,<br>That should death, unwish&rsquo;d for, come,<br>Pointing to the dreary tomb,<br>I might cry, in sprightly tone,<br>&ldquo;Here&rsquo;s my ransom, Death! begone!&rdquo;<br>But, alas! since well I know<br>Life cannot be purchased so,<br>Why indulge the useless sigh?<br>Fate decrees that all shall die.<br>Vainly to our wealth we trust,<br>Poor or wealthy — die we must.<br>Present joys then let me share,<br>Rosy wine to banish care;<br>Cheerful friends that faithful prove,<br>Beauty&rsquo;s smiles and blissful love."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE XXIV. — LIFE TO BE ENJOYED."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Born a mortal; doom&rsquo;d to tread<br>Life&rsquo;s rough path of pain and wo,"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "All the past with ease is read,<br>But the future who can know?"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Hence! away, distracting cares,<br>Make no fellowship with me;<br>Point not to my silvering hairs,<br>You and I shall ne&rsquo;er agree."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Ere fate forbid all further joy,<br>First amid the festive throng,<br>Bacchus shall my hours employ<br>With mirth, and dance, and joyous song."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE XXV. — THE CURE FOR CARE."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "When with gloomy griefs oppress&rsquo;d,<br>Wine can charm those griefs to rest;<br>Toil and trouble, care and wo,<br>I&rsquo;m determined ne&rsquo;er to know.<br>Though in care my life were pass&rsquo;d,<br>Cruel death would come at last.<br>Shall I ever anxious grieve?<br>Shall I thus myself deceive?<br>No! we&rsquo;ll drain the rosy bowl,<br>&rsquo;Tis a cordial for the soul;<br>&rsquo;Tis a charm that lulls to rest<br>Every anxious, aching breast."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE XXVI. — IN PRAISE OF WINE."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "When the nectar&rsquo;d bowl I drain<br>Gloomy cares forego their reign;<br>Richer than the Lydian king,<br>Hymns of love and joy I sing;<br>Ivy wreaths my temples twine,<br>And, while careless I recline,<br>While bright scenes my vision greet,<br>Tread the world beneath my feet."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Fill the cup, my trusty page,<br>Anacreon, the blithe and sage,<br>As his maxim, ever said,<br>Those slain by wine are noble dead."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE XXVII. — THE SAME SUBJECT."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "When the generous god of wine,<br>Bacchus, son of Jove divine,<br>Frees my soul from anxious care,<br>Fills my breast and revels there,<br>Then I lead the mazy dance,<br>Rapt in pleasure&rsquo;s giddy trance.<br>Oh! what transports then I prove — <br>Sweet the joys of wine and love!<br>Music breathes its softest strains,<br>Venus too with Bacchus reigns.<br>Thus, with wine and beauty bless&rsquo;d,<br>Thus I charm my cares to rest,<br>Ever joyous, blithe, and gay,<br>Dance the happy hours away."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE XXVIII. — ON HIS MISTRESS."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Best of painters, lend thy aid,<br>Draw the lines of light and shade;<br>Master of the Rhodian art,<br>Paint the charmer of my heart;"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Absent though the maiden be,<br>Beauties I&rsquo;ll describe to thee,<br>Thou, undazzled, ne&rsquo;er couldst see.<br>Paint her dark and glossy hair,<br>Flowing down her neck so fair:<br>Further yet I must presume,<br>Let it seem to breathe perfume.<br>Her iv&rsquo;ry forehead next thy care,<br>Shining mid her jet-black hair;<br>Let thy utmost skill be seen<br>In the dainty space between,<br>Where by sable archers cross&rsquo;d,<br>Where the less&rsquo;ning shade is lost.<br>Let her liquid eye of fire,<br>Like Minerva&rsquo;s, awe inspire;<br>With Cytherea&rsquo;s softness too<br>Temper the celestial blue;"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Paint her lovely cheek and nose,<br>Blending milk with blush of rose;<br>Paint her pretty, pouting lips,<br>Where the bee its honey sips,<br>Where Persuasion sits and smiles,<br>With a thousand winning wiles.<br>Every pleasing grace must deck<br>Her pretty dimpled chin and neck;<br>And let nameless beauties dwell<br>In her bosom&rsquo;s gentle swell.<br>In a thin and purple dress<br>Veil this form of loveliness:<br>Her body hide, her shape express.<br>Enough! no further proof I seek,<br>She lives — she breathes — soft! did she speak?"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE XXX. — CUPID TAKEN PRISONER."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Cupid, once, was rambling found<br>On the Muses&rsquo; hallow&rsquo;d ground;<br>Straight they weave a rosy chain,<br>And the little god detain.<br>Him to Beauty soon they gave,<br>Mighty Love is Beauty&rsquo;s slave.<br>Cytherea ransoms brought,<br>To release her son she sought;<br>But no fee, no ransom now,<br>The happy captive will allow.<br>Love hath learn&rsquo;d his art too well,<br>And with Beauty still will dwell."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE XXXI. — PLEASING PHRENSY."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Yes! let me — let me drain the bowl,<br>And pour its pleasures on my soul;<br>Let Bacchus now his reign employ,<br>Till reason reels, oppress&rsquo;d with joy.<br>Orestes, by the furies led,<br>Barefooted to the mountains fled.<br>Alcmæon too, in frantic mood,<br>Like him was stain&rsquo;d with mother&rsquo;s blood;<br>But I disclaim such dreadful deeds,<br>My madness from my joy proceeds.<br>Then bring the bowl, I cry again,<br>Who shall that maddening joy restrain?"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "When Hercules went mad of yore,<br>The Iphitean bow he bore;<br>His rattling quiver&rsquo;s dreadful sound<br>Spread awe and consternation round.<br>Great Ajax, too, when madness raged,<br>Whole hosts of fancied Greeks engaged;<br>When, grasping fierce his seven-fold shield,<br>With Hector&rsquo;s sword he sought the field.<br>But though with wine I mad should be,<br>May no such fury seize on me!<br>No dreadful bow or sword I bear,<br>A flowery garland decks my hair.<br>This brimming bowl shall crown my bliss,<br>Then welcome madness such as this!"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE XXXII. — ON THE NUMBER OF HIS MISTRESSES."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "If thou canst number o&rsquo;er to me<br>Every leaf on every tree,<br>Or count the ceaseless waves that roar<br>Against the billow-beaten shore,<br>Thou sufficient skill hast proved;<br>Thou shalt count the names I&rsquo;ve loved.<br>At Athens first, Minerva&rsquo;s town,<br>Full five-and-thirty write me down;<br>But oh! at Corinth, rich and fair,<br>What hosts of loved ones had I there!"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "For beauteous nymphs it bears the sway,<br>For none so beauteous sure as they.<br>Next, my lovely Lesbians tell,<br>Ionians, Carians, those that dwell<br>At far-famed Rhodes — you may in all<br>The trifling sum two thousand call.<br>What! think&rsquo;st thou that I yet have done?<br>Resume thy tablets — one by one,<br>I&rsquo;ll count thee o&rsquo;er my Syrian fair,<br>And Egypt too must claim a share;<br>And fertile Creta yet remains,<br>Where Love his empire still maintains.<br>The dark-eyed nymphs that shared my flame,<br>At Spain and Afric, shall I name?<br>To sultry India&rsquo;s farthest pole,<br>Whose dusky charms have fired my soul!"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE XXXIII. — ON A SWALLOW."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Pretty, twittering, fickle guest,<br>Here you build your summer nest;<br>But, ere storms deface the sky,<br>Back to warmer worlds you fly;"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "To Memphis, or the banks of Nile,<br>Where bright suns for ever smile.<br>But, alas! nor peace nor rest<br>Dwells within my hapless breast:<br>Love still builds and hatches there,<br>Full-fledged loves for flight prepare;<br>Some, unhatch&rsquo;d, yet quiet dwell,<br>Some just struggling through the shell,<br>While their ceaseless chirping noise<br>Every hope of peace destroys.<br>Some usurp the parent&rsquo;s care,<br>And the younger nestlings rear;<br>These, when grown, will young ones breed;<br>Others still to them succeed.<br>Thus, alas! what hope remains — <br>What can ease my bosom&rsquo;s pains,<br>Since within its secret cell<br>Loves innumerable dwell?"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE XXXIV. — TO HIS MISTRESS."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Fly me not, thou scornful fair,<br>Why reject me so?<br>Is it that my scanty hair<br>Is whiter than the snow?"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Beauty&rsquo;s blooming flower is thine,<br>And on thy cheek it glows;<br>But do not lilies brighter shine<br>When blended with the rose?"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE XXXV. — ON A PICTURE REPRESENTING EUROPA."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "This bull, my boy, is surely meant<br>The mighty Jove to represent,<br>Since on his back he seems to bear<br>Through pathless seas a Tyrian fair.<br>With steady strength he stems the tide,<br>His hoofs the billows dash aside;<br>For sure no other bull but he<br>Would from his lovely heifers flee,<br>And tempt the dangers of the sea."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE XXXVI. — LIFE TO BE ENJOYED."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Why prate to me of critic rules,<br>And jargon of the jangling schools?<br>Your learned dogmas, prithee, spare,<br>They&rsquo;re useless all — not worth my care.<br>I&rsquo;ll hear thee gladly, canst thou tell<br>The happy art of living well;<br>How best to mix the sparkling wine,<br>To make the mellow draught divine;"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "How best to please the lovely fair,<br>For this indeed is worth my care.<br>Alas! each day, each hour I know,<br>My hoary locks still whiter grow:<br>Then bring the goblet — let me drink,<br>&lsquo;Twill only make me sad to think<br>How near, how very near the day<br>When, mix&rsquo;d with earth and kindred clay,<br>My soul no more shall taste of joy,<br>Nor schemes of bliss my mind employ."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE XXXVII. — ON THE SPRING."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "The newborn Spring awakes the flowers,<br>And bathes their buds in dewy showers:<br>The roses bloom, the Graces wear<br>Fresh flowery garlands in their hair.<br>How sleeps the sea in placid rest!<br>No storms disturb its peaceful breast;<br>But oft upon its surface green<br>The diving duck is sporting seen.<br>From distant skies now comes the crane<br>To seek her well-known haunts again;"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "The smiling sun resumes his sway,<br>And drives the dismal clouds away;<br>The teeming&rsquo; earth is big with fruits,<br>Forth into day the olive shoots;<br>Rich, juicy clusters deck the vine,<br>Which soon shall ripen into wine:<br>The charming sight with joy I see,<br>To Bacchus welcome — and to me."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE XXXVIII. — ON HIMSELF."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "True, ah! true, I&rsquo;m growing old;<br>Why should not the truth be told?<br>Still, from youths I never shrink<br>When the business is to drink.<br>When the joyous troop advance,<br>Still I join the merry dance:<br>I no useless sceptre bear;<br>But on high my bottle rear.<br>Should the grape some hero fire,<br>Should he wars and fights desire,<br>Let him fight then, if he please,<br>I prefer my peaceful ease.<br>Bring me, then, my gentle page,<br>Wine that glows with strength and age."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "True, I&rsquo;m old; but you shall see<br>Old Silenus, full of glee,<br>Acted to the life by me."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE XXXIX. — ON HIMSELF."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "When the rosy wine inspires,<br>Every muse my bosom fires,<br>All the joys of love and song<br>Cheer my heart and tune my tongue."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "When the joys of wine I share,<br>Farewell every anxious care;<br>Sportive winds my sorrows sweep<br>To the restless, roaring deep."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "When I drain the spacious bowl,<br>Bacchus charms my ravish&rsquo;d soul;<br>Perfumed gales from beds of flowers<br>Bathe in bliss the happy hours."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "When with rosy garlands crown&rsquo;d,<br>The social cup I pass around;<br>Rapt in fancy&rsquo;s airy dream,<br>Peaceful pleasures are my theme."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "When I quaff the grape&rsquo;s rich juice,<br>Bathed in liquid sweets profuse,<br>Venus claims my votive strain,<br>Chloe fills my arms again."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "When the joy-inspiring draught<br>Frees my soul from anxious thought,<br>Graver thoughts I fling away,<br>Sporting with the young and gay."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "When I glow with generous wine,<br>Life&rsquo;s real blessings all are mine,<br>Joys beyond the reach of fate — <br>Death is sure in every state."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE XL. — CUPID WOUNDED."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Young Cupid, once, in luckless hour,<br>Saw and pluck&rsquo;d his favourite flower,<br>A blooming rose — whose leaves among<br>A bee that slept his finger stung.<br>Loud he scream&rsquo;d with sudden pain,<br>Stamp&rsquo;d and sobb&rsquo;d — then scream&rsquo;d again.<br>He runs — he flies through mead and grove,<br>To seek the beauteous Queen of Love.<br>&ldquo;Ah me! mamma, I&rsquo;m kill&rsquo;d,&rdquo; he cries,<br>&ldquo;Thy child, thy own dear Cupid dies!<br>For, as I play&rsquo;d on yonder plain,<br>A winged serpent — ah! what pain!<br>A thing the ploughmen call a bee,<br>With dart of poison wounded me.&rdquo;<br>Fair Venus, smiling, thus replies:<br>&ldquo;Oh dry those pretty pearly eyes;"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Think if a little insect&rsquo;s sting<br>Such painful smart to Cupid bring,<br>Oh, what must their keen anguish be<br>Who&rsquo;re wounded to the heart by thee!&rdquo;"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE XLI. — THE BANQUET OF WINE."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Come, let the mantling cups be crown&rsquo;d,<br>And let the jovial song go round.<br>To Bacchus still the strain prolong,<br>Who taught the dance, and loves the song.<br>Companion blithe with Cupid seen,<br>Beloved alike by beauty&rsquo;s queen;<br>The father, he, of joy and mirth,<br>To him the Graces owe their birth.<br>He heals the wounds of pain and grief,<br>In him the wretched find relief.<br>When blooming youths present the bowl<br>Sweet joys alone possess the soul;<br>And, borne aloft, our sorrows fly<br>On swift-wing&rsquo;d storms that sweep the sky.<br>Then let us anxious thoughts dismiss,<br>And pledge the cup to scenes of bliss;<br>For what avails heart-rending care,<br>Since mortal man is sorrow&rsquo;s heir.<br>How short his life&rsquo;s uncertain date!<br>Unknown and dark his future state.<br>But when the brimming bowl I drain<br>I love to dance along the plain,"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "With sweet perfumes to bathe my hair<br>And frolic with the young and fair.<br>Let anxious idiots still despise<br>The joys which wiser men will prize.<br>Then, while the jovial cup goes round,<br>To Bacchus let the song resound."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE XLII. — ON HIMSELF."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "A friend to mirth and harmless sport,<br>I love the dance which Bacchus taught.<br>I dearly love to wake the lyre<br>When wine or love my lays inspire;<br>But dearer, sweeter joys I prove,<br>When with gay smiling maids I rove;<br>While hyacinths sweet odours breathe,<br>And round my brows their blossoms wreath,<br>My heart from envious thoughts is free,<br>And even Envy still spares me:<br>From Slander&rsquo;s venom&rsquo;d tongue I fly,<br>And shun the shafts of calumny.<br>Fierce quarrels o&rsquo;er the festive board<br>My honest heart has e&rsquo;er abhorr&rsquo;d:<br>But, dancing to the lute&rsquo;s soft strain,<br>I love to join the blooming train.<br>Oh! let us banish barb&rsquo;rous strife,<br>And lead a happy, peaceful life."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE XLIII. — ON THE GRASSHOPPER."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Happy insect! all agree<br>None can be more bless&rsquo;d than thee;<br>Thou, for joy and pleasure born,<br>Sipp&rsquo;st the honey&rsquo;d dew of morn.<br>Happier than the sceptred king,<br>Mid the boughs we hear thee sing.<br>All the season&rsquo;s varied store,<br>All thy little eyes explore,<br>Fruits that tempt, and flowers that shine,<br>Happy insect! all are thine.<br>Injuring nothing, blamed by none,<br>Farmers love thee — pretty one!<br>All rejoice thy voice to hear<br>Singing blithe when summer&rsquo;s near.<br>Thee the tuneful Muses love,<br>Sweetly chirping in the grove;<br>Thee the great Apollo bless&rsquo;d<br>With a voice above the rest.<br>Thou from wasting age art free,<br>Time has naught to do with thee.<br>Skilful creature, child of song,<br>Though to earth thou dost belong,<br>Free from Nature&rsquo;s woes and pains,<br>Free from flesh, or blood-fill&rsquo;d veins,<br>Happy thing! thou seem&rsquo;st to me<br>Almost a little god to be!"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE XLIV. — THE DREAM."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "I dream&rsquo;d, that over earth and sky,<br>Possess&rsquo;d with wings, I seem&rsquo;d to fly;<br>While Love pursued with swiftest pace,<br>And soon o&rsquo;ertook me in the chase;<br>Though at his little feet were hung<br>Large leaden weights, that loosely swung.<br>&ldquo;What can this vision mean?&rdquo; I cried;<br>&ldquo;It surely may be thus applied, — <br>That I, who once could freely rove<br>Through all the flowery paths of love,<br>Who laugh&rsquo;d at lovers and their pains,<br>Am fettered now with stronger chains.&rdquo;"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE XLV. — CUPID’S DARTS."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "The rugged mate of love&rsquo;s soft queen<br>Was at the Lemnian forges seen;<br>And while their fires intensely glow,<br>Was forging darts for Cupid&rsquo;s bow;<br>Sharp-pointed shafts of polish&rsquo;d steel,<br>Which human hearts so keenly feel.<br>The gentle Venus, for her part, <br>In honey dipp&rsquo;d each finish&rsquo;d dart;<br>But cruel Cupid took them all,<br>And steep&rsquo;d their barbed points in gall.<br>Returning from the battle rude,<br>The mighty Mars their business view&rsquo;d;<br>And, leaning on his massy spear,<br>&ldquo;What use,&rdquo; he cried, with scornful sneer,<br>&ldquo;These puny darts — these trifling toys — <br>Mere playthings — only fit for boys?&rdquo;<br>&ldquo;Hold!&rdquo; Cupid cries, &ldquo;here&rsquo;s one — try this,<br>You&rsquo;ll find it not so much amiss;<br>&rsquo;Tis strongly made; and, for its size,<br>Its weight will cause you much surprise.&rdquo;<br>The god received it. Venus tried<br>To check her laugh, and turn&rsquo;d aside;<br>But Mars, with sudden grief possess&rsquo;d,<br>Cried, groaning from his inmost breast,<br>&ldquo;This little shaft gives wondrous pain;<br>Here — take it — take it back again.&rdquo;<br>&ldquo;Nay, Mars, I give it with good will;<br>Pray keep the pretty plaything still.&rdquo;"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE XLVI. — THE POWER OF GOLD."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "A thousand pains we lovers prove,<br>Still what were life devoid of love?<br>But ah! what wo, when doom&rsquo;d to mourn<br>The love that never meets return!<br>In vain we boast of noble birth,<br>And vain is wisdom, wit, or worth,<br>Since sordid wealth alone is sought,<br>And even love with gold is bought.<br>Oh may he sleep in endless night,<br>Who brought the shining plague to light;<br>Who first gave worth to useless ore,<br>And taught mankind to sigh for more!<br>Gold breaks through every sacred tie,<br>And bids a friend or brother die;<br>The fruitful source of kindred strife,<br>Gold would not spare a parent&rsquo;s life.<br>Long wars and murders, crimes untold,<br>All spring from cursed thirst of gold;"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "And I by sad experience know<br>&rsquo;Tis gold that works the lover&rsquo;s wo!"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE XLVII. — YOUNG OLD AGE."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "I love the cheerful, blithesome sage,<br>Whose temper ne&rsquo;er betrays his age.<br>I love the youth that dances well,<br>To music of the sounding shell.<br>But when an aged youth like me<br>Can join the dance with sportive glee,<br>Though age in hoary locks appears,<br>His heart is young, despite his years."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE XLVIII. — HAPPY LIFE."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Oh! for the harp, the harp of fire,<br>That godlike Homer strung:<br>But ah! on such a blood-stain&rsquo;d lyre<br>Could love&rsquo;s soft notes be sung?"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "No! let the measured cups be brought,<br>And from this scroll divine<br>I&rsquo;ll read the laws which Bacchus taught<br>To votaries of the wine."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Then warm in heart, but wisely gay,<br>I&rsquo;ll join the sportive throng;"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "With joy the merry harp I&rsquo;ll play,<br>And thrill the jovial song."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE XLIX. — TO A PAINTER."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Dear artist, while I wake the string,<br>Paint thou the lovely scenes I sing:<br>First, let my fix&rsquo;d, delighted eyes,<br>Behold a well-built city rise;<br>And with inventive skill portray<br>Its people happy, blithe, and gay.<br>Describe the Bacchanalian throng,<br>Engaged in festive dance and song;<br>Where, while the shrill-voiced pipe is mute,<br>Is heard the softly-breathing flute.<br>And if the crowded space permit,<br>To make the blissful scene complete,<br>Let happy pairs be seen to rove,<br>Intent on life&rsquo;s best bus&rsquo;ness — love."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE L. — ON BACCHUS."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "See! the youthful god descends;<br>Bacchus, who the youth befriends,"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Strings his nerves, strong toil to bear,<br>Courage gives to win the fair;<br>Graceful ease and skill bestows,<br>When the vigorous dancer glows.<br>In his hand behold he bears<br>An antidote for human cares;<br>Bless&rsquo;d with which poor mortals gam<br>Pleasure&rsquo;s draught unmix&rsquo;d with pain.<br>He preserves the future wine,<br>While the crimson clusters shine,<br>Ere the juice is taught to flow,<br>Sweet assuager of our wo.<br>Wine, the cure of every ill,<br>Proves the best physician still;<br>All its happy patients find<br>Health of body, ease of mind.<br>Sound in mind — in body sound,<br>While the rolling year goes round,<br>Till the grapes again appear,<br>Med&rsquo;cine for another year."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE LI. — ON A MEDAL REPRESENTING VENUS."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "What matchless skill! what art divine<br>On this bright silver medal shine!<br>On every side; above, below,<br>The floods of ocean seem to flow;"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "While softly gliding, calm and clear,<br>The undulating waves appear.<br>Some heav&rsquo;n-taught genius, in its flight.<br>Has dared attempt the wondrous sight<br>Of Venus, love&rsquo;s soft deity,<br>Emerging from the silver sea.<br>What bright and dazzling beauties rise<br>To charm the gazer&rsquo;s ravish&rsquo;d eyes!<br>And those the jealous waves conceal,<br>Sure none but impious hands reveal.<br>She, like some sea-flower, fresh and gay,<br>Shines glittering on her watery way.<br>Where&rsquo;er the lovely goddess swims,<br>Obsequious billows kiss her limbs;<br>Now rise above, now sink below<br>Her rose-bud breasts and neck of snow.<br>As virgin lilies brighter show<br>Amid the dark-leaved violet&rsquo;s glow,<br>So through the dark-blue wave is seen<br>The beauteous form of love&rsquo;s dear queen.<br>See, gayly sporting at her side,<br>Young laughing Loves on dolphins ride,<br>And o&rsquo;er the silvery surface glide.<br>The crooked natives of the deep,<br>With wanton curve and bounding leap,<br>Attend the goddess in her tram,<br>Where&rsquo;er she smiling skims the main."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE LII. — ON THE VINTAGE."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Now ripen&rsquo;d by the genial sun,<br>The grapes are glean&rsquo;d; the sports begun.<br>See youths and smiling virgins bear<br>The purple produce of the year;<br>In vats the luscious burden lies,<br>And home the modest maiden hies:<br>For joyous youths alone remain,<br>With blood-red juice their limbs to stain,<br>To crush the cluster&rsquo;s bloomy pride,<br>And revel in the crimson tide.<br>Then loud they raise the vintage hymn,<br>When foaming o&rsquo;er the vessel&rsquo;s brim<br>They view the joy-inspiring juice,<br>Which Bacchus sends them for their use.<br>Should hoary age inhale the draught,<br>His youth renew&rsquo;d, at least in thought,<br>His tott&rsquo;ring, trembling limbs advance,<br>And try the long-forgotten dance.<br>But when the youth its influence feels,<br>When wine prevails, and reason reels;<br>When wandering through the lonely grove<br>His heart beats high with hopes of love;<br>If there, beneath the secret shade,<br>He chance to spy some lovely maid,<br>Who, press&rsquo;d by sleep&rsquo;s invading pow&rsquo;r,"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Lies slumb&rsquo;ring mid the leafy bower,<br>Herself the fairest, frailest flower, — <br>Before the startled maid can rouse<br>He breathes his hasty, burning vows,<br>And while his breast with Bacchus glows,<br>His lawless love he dares propose.<br>In vain the angry fair denies,<br>He better reads her telltale eyes;<br>And sure of victory ere &rsquo;tis won,<br>His eager suit he urges on;<br>And when his soft persuasion fails,<br>Rude, boisterous Bacchus oft prevails:<br>And thus the wanton god decoys<br>The youth to wild intemperate joys."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE LIII. — ON THE ROSE."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Thou, my friend, shalt sweep the string,<br>I, in softest strains will sing,<br>While its fragrance round us flows,<br>The queen of flowers — the lovely rose.<br>Its perfumed breath ascends the skies<br>On every gentle gale that sighs:<br>Its sweets descend to earth again,<br>Alike beloved by gods and men.<br>When Spring awakes the slumbering flowers,<br>And music breathes amid the bowers,<br>Thee, darling gem, the Graces wear<br>Intwined amid their flowing hair;<br>And rosy wreaths alone may dress<br>The queen of love and loveliness.<br>In every song and fable known<br>The Muses claim thee as their own."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Thou bidd&rsquo;st thy blooming sweetness blow<br>In thorny paths of pain and wo.<br>But, oh! what joy, when bless&rsquo;d we rove<br>Through rosy bowers, and dream of love;<br>While bliss on every breeze is borne,<br>To pluck the rose without the thorn;<br>With gentlest touch its leaves to press,<br>And raise it to our soft caress!<br>Oh! thou art still the poet&rsquo;s theme,<br>And thee a welcome guest we deem,<br>To grace our feasts and deck our hair,<br>When Bacchus bids us banish care.<br>E&rsquo;en Nature does thy beauties prize,<br>She steals thy teints to paint the skies;<br>For rosy-finger&rsquo;d is the morn<br>With which the crimson veil is drawn.<br>The lovely nymphs we always deck<br>With rosy arms and rosy neck,"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "And roseate teints are ever seen<br>To bloom the cheeks of beauty&rsquo;s queen.<br>Its power to sooth the pangs of pain<br>Physicians try, nor try in vain;<br>And e&rsquo;en when life and hope are fled<br>Its deathless scent embalms the dead:<br>For, though its withering charms decay,<br>And, one by one, all fade away,<br>Its grateful smell the rose retains,<br>And redolent of youth remains.<br>But, lyrist, let it next be sung<br>From whence this precious treasure sprung — <br>When first from ocean&rsquo;s dewy spray<br>Fair Venus rose to upper day;<br>When, fearful to the powers above,<br>The armed Pallas sprung from Jove;<br>&rsquo;Twas then they say the jealous earth<br>First gave the lovely stranger birth.<br>A drop of pure nectareous dew<br>From heaven the bless&rsquo;d immortals threw;<br>A while it trembled on the thorn,<br>And then the lovely rose was born.<br>To Bacchus they the flower assign,<br>And roses still his brows intwine."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE LIV. — ON HIMSELF."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "While I view the youthful throng,<br>Fancy whispers I am young!<br>To the merry dance I fly,<br>Who so gay, so brisk as I?<br>Haste, Cybele, bring me flowers,<br>Bring sweet roses from the bowers;"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Quick a graceful garland twine,<br>Youthful vigour still is mine.<br>Hateful, hoary age, away!<br>Let me sport with striplings gay;<br>Bring the bright autumnal bowl — <br>Age can ne&rsquo;er subdue the soul.<br>Still I raise the cheerful strain,<br>Still the brimming bowl I drain;<br>Still with native humour gay,<br>Sport the happy hours away!"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE LV. — THE LOVER’S MARK."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "The courser bears a brand of fire,<br>To mark his owner, or his sire;<br>The turban, twisted round his brows,<br>The fiery foreign Parthian shows;<br>And I by instinct sure can tell<br>The lover&rsquo;s mark — I know it well:<br>For love in vain concealment tries,<br>The soul peeps through the telltale eyes."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE LVI. — ON HIS OLD AGE."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Alas! my youth, my joys have fled,<br>The snows of age have bleach&rsquo;d my head.<br>Tedious, toothless, trembling age,<br>Must now alone my thoughts engage!<br>Adieu, ye joys which once I knew — <br>To life, to love, to all, adieu!<br>Henceforth, unhappy! doom&rsquo;d to know<br>Tormenting fears of future wo!<br>Oh! how my soul with horror shrinks<br>Whene&rsquo;er my startled fancy thinks<br>Of Pluto&rsquo;s dark and dreary cave,<br>The chill, the cheerless, gaping grave!<br>When death&rsquo;s cold hand has closed these eyes<br>And stifled life&rsquo;s last struggling sighs,<br>In darkness and in dust must I,<br>Alas! for ever — ever lie!"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE LVII. — THAT MODERATION ENHANCES ENJOYMENT."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Haste! haste thee, boy, and bring the bowl,<br>To quench this fever of the soul;<br>The copious stream with skill combine,<br>Add ten parts water, five of wine;"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "The cooling draught will thirst assuage,<br>Nor in the breast too fiercely rage.<br>Oh cease, my friends, for shame, give o&rsquo;er<br>These clamorous shouts, this deaf&rsquo;ning roar:<br>This Scythian scene all peace destroys;<br>Turns joy to madness, mirth to noise.<br>Let cheerful temperance rule the soul,<br>The best ingredient in the bowl."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE LVIII. — LOVE IN THE HEART."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "As once, amid the rosy bowers,<br>I wove a crown of fairest flowers,<br>Love, little urchin, lurking sly<br>Beneath the leaves I chanced to spy;<br>Around his wings the wreath I twine,<br>And plunge him in a cup of wine:<br>Then love, in each delicious draught,<br>I from the foaming goblet quaff&rsquo;d.<br>Oh! still he moves his fluttering wings,<br>Still to my heart strange transport brings."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE LIX. — ON HIMSELF."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Methought, in sleep&rsquo;s delightful trance,<br>I saw Anacreon advance;"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "The tuneful Teian, skill&rsquo;d to sing<br>The lays of love on warbling string.<br>I hasten&rsquo;d to his kind embrace,<br>And kiss&rsquo;d his sweetly smiling face.<br>Though somewhat old, he seemed to wage<br>Successful war with spiteful age:<br>For love still beam&rsquo;d in each bright eye,<br>And from his lips there seem&rsquo;d to fly<br>Sweet gales of rich and rosy wine,<br>Which shed a fragrance quite divine.<br>His slow and staggering steps were stay&rsquo;d<br>By laughing Cupid&rsquo;s kindly aid.<br>The garland that intwined his hair<br>The bard unbound and bade me wear.<br>Anacreon&rsquo;s burning soul it breathed,<br>And I with it my brows enwreathed.<br>E&rsquo;er since my heart is doom&rsquo;d to prove<br>The pleasing pains of lasting love."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "ODE LX. — ON THE SPRING."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "How sweet through sunny meads to stray,<br>With Flora&rsquo;s rich profusion gay,<br>While Zephyr breathes its softest sighs,<br>And mingled perfumes round us rise!<br>How sweet beneath the secret shade,<br>By the vine&rsquo;s broad foliage made,<br>With some loved fair to pass the day,<br>And talk th&rsquo; unheeded hours away!"}],[]);})();